<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Device administrator policy for the driver app.

  Declaring a device admin is what prevents casual uninstallation: Android
  refuses to remove an app while it holds active admin rights, so a driver
  cannot simply long-press the icon and delete it.

  It is a speed bump rather than a lock. The rights can be revoked from
  Settings > Security > Device admin apps, and uninstall then proceeds
  normally. Genuine enforcement needs the device enrolled as fully managed
  (see docs/device-management.md); this raises the bar and, more usefully,
  makes removal a deliberate act that the server can notice.

  The policies requested are deliberately minimal — only what is needed to
  hold admin status. Nothing here locks, wipes or restricts the phone, so
  activating it cannot brick a driver's device.
-->
<device-admin>
    <uses-policies>
        <force-lock />
    </uses-policies>
</device-admin>
